{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:labor:fermi","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:b6accdca9a3605ecdc5fcd82b9902bc058fae284f998a248f45bfe3d29b377dc","modelForecastIds":["sha256:875628ef8184d435ac91f0209664fe150b8ecb7337b3b72fd88297075eefcf64"],"modelUse":"consulted","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.31},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"CONJUNCT A: GDP-weighted OECD-38 core averages >=2.50%/yr over 2027-36. Additive build off a 2.00% target anchor, each term in pp/yr: +0.20 care/construction Baumol relative-price term (F+Q services and housing-maintenance are ~13-15% of the core basket; a 1.5 pp relative-price wedge x 0.14 = 0.21); +0.25 fiscal-dominance tolerance as the net interest claim goes 2%->3% of GDP (the realized historical tolerance band is 0.2-0.5 pp of target overshoot under that kind of interest burden); +0.25 non-tradable labour scarcity (labour supply -0.1%/yr against in-person service demand +1.5%/yr pushes service ULC ~0.4 pp above the 2%-consistent path, x the ~0.6 service weight); +0.08 grid/power pass-through (the 635 TWh OECD+China datacenter increment implied by AI-Q3 P50 raises industrial/retail power, ~3% of core directly and indirectly); -0.09 AI deflation in tradable services (if AI cuts the price of the ~6% of the basket that is outsourceable back-office/support by 15% over 10 yr); -0.15 imported goods deflation from Chinese excess capacity, which is the SAME object as conjunct B. Sum = 2.54%. Over a 10-year mean, sigma ~0.35, so P(>=2.50) = ~0.55. MARGINAL A = 0.55. CONJUNCT B: China headline CPI averages <1.50%/yr over 2027-36. Decomposition: start point 0.0-0.3% (2024-26 run rate). Structural suppressors are quantified in 2A: worker/retiree falls 4.4->2.8 and pension subsidies already absorb 10.1% of fiscal expenditure, so the pre-retirement cohort faces an underfunded system and raises precautionary saving (a direct demand suppressor), on top of property deleveraging and manufacturing excess capacity. Arithmetic test: a linear path from 0.3% in 2027 to 2.0% in 2036 gives a 10-yr mean of 1.15%; reaching a 1.50% mean requires hitting target well before 2033, i.e. a decisive reflation (large hukou/safety-net transfer, or RMB depreciation importing inflation, or a food shock). MARGINAL B = 0.66. CORRELATION: NEGATIVE, rho ~ -0.20, and this is the structural heart of the conjunction. The -0.15 pp term I used in conjunct A IS Chinese exported deflation; the mechanism that satisfies B (Chinese demand deficiency) mechanically pushes OECD core DOWN, while a world of global cost-push (energy, fragmentation, commodities) that lifts OECD core to 2.5%+ would also lift Chinese CPI toward 1.5%+. The question asks for a divergence, and the divergence is partly self-undermining. P(B|A) = 0.66 x 0.85 = 0.56. MULTIPLICATION: 0.55 x 0.56 = 0.308 -> 31. The conjunction discount here is modest in size but correctly signed: the naive product of the two \"obvious\" marginals (both individually more-likely-than-not for B) is pulled down by the negative dependence, not up. (Simulation refused for CQ2 per adapter: \"not representable - the model has no price level\". The model is entirely real; no number above comes from it.)","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:55.319Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD Health at a Glance and national LTC workforce statistics show OECD-38 health-and-social-work (ISIC Q) employment growing >=2.5%/yr over 2026-2029 (versus the ~1.5-1.8%/yr my rationing view implies) WITH reported LTC vacancy rates falling below 8% in at least five of the G7 plus Korea.\",\"by_when\":\"Mid-2030 (on 2029 reference-year data).\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My central claim — that the care bottleneck clears by quantity rationing rather than by price, and that the J/K/M-to-Q conversion rate is ~7% of the needed flow — is wrong. The queue is clearing, so hypothesis plank (a) is far better than 7 (move it to 35-45). CQ1 rises 6-9 pp (to ~18-20, since both conjuncts benefit: a clearing care sector absorbs displaced workers AND bids wages). Move 5-8 points from CQ6-C to CQ6-B and A, since the supply wall is the main C generator. Raise CQ3 P50 by about +0.15 pp to ~1.65%, via a smaller Baumol composition drag and fewer family caregivers withdrawing from the labour force.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US BLS and Eurostat sectoral median hourly earnings series show real median pay in ISIC F + Q outpacing ISIC J + K + M by >=4.5 percentage points cumulatively over 2025-2030 — i.e. on pace for the +10 pp the CQ1 conjunct needs by 2036 — with the gap widening rather than flat.\",\"by_when\":\"Q3 2031 (2030 reference-year releases).\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My conjunct-B marginal of 0.18 was too low, chiefly because my selection-bias argument (AI exit from the lower half of J/K/M mechanically raising that sector's measured median) was weaker than the scarcity bid. Raise the marginal to ~0.45, roughly doubling CQ1 to 22-25. Add 4-6 points to CQ6-C and take them from B, since a genuine care-wage spiral is the stagflationary-bottleneck generator. Raise my CQ2 conjunct-A marginal from 0.55 to ~0.68 (the Baumol term is running above my +0.20 pp), lifting CQ2 to ~38.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Philippine IT-BPM headcount (IBPAP annual reporting) falls below 1.4M (from ~1.7M+) AND Philippine total remittance inflows fail to exceed $48B/yr — i.e. the virtual leg contracts hard while the physical-migration and remittance channel does not expand to compensate.\",\"by_when\":\"End-2031 (on full-year 2031 BSP and IBPAP data).\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The migration valve is NOT operating at the 400-650k/yr scale I assumed, and the net spillover sign I asserted is wrong. Raise hypothesis plank (b) from 12 to ~40, since SEVERELY and BLOCKS would then be close to right for the Philippines. Simultaneously — and this is the coupled consequence, not a separate one — a closed migration valve means OECD care wages are no longer capped from abroad, so CQ1's conjunct B gains (marginal 0.18 -> 0.30, CQ1 to ~16) and CQ6-C gains 4-5 points from B. Cut CQ3 P50 by about -0.05 pp on the lost OECD hours.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"The G7 GDP-weighted 10-year sovereign real yield, constructed on my published weights (0.50 US / 0.11 JP / 0.10 DE / 0.08 UK / 0.08 FR / 0.07 IT / 0.06 CA) against 10y nominal minus 10y inflation swap, averages above 2.6% for any rolling twelve months, WITH Japan's 10y real above +1.0%.\",\"by_when\":\"End-2031.\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The financial-repression wedge is not binding and the term premium is at or above my high end. Raise CQ4 to P50 2.6-2.8% with P10/P90 about 1.8/3.6, and note this is the cleanest single test of my CQ4 because it discriminates the repression term from r* directly. Raise CQ5 by 6-8 pp (to ~25), since an unrepressed, high-term-premium duration market is precisely the regime in which bonds fail to hedge an equity drawdown and the MOVE mean clears 105. Shift 4-6 points from CQ6-B to CQ6-C. Cut CQ3 P50 by -0.1 pp on the higher hurdle rate applied to long-duration grid and datacenter capex.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD-38 harmonized unemployment prints above 6.0% for four consecutive quarters at any point before end-2032 WHILE GDP-weighted OECD-38 core inflation stays above 2.5% over the same four quarters (the conjunction matters: unemployment alone would be an ordinary cycle).\",\"by_when\":\"End-2032.\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The AI mismatch channel is materially larger than the +0.56 pp I computed, and the shrinking-labour-force pull of -0.7 pp is not offsetting it — i.e. displaced cognitive workers are neither re-absorbed nor retiring. Cut CQ1 to 4-6 (conjunct A's 0.52 collapses toward 0.15). Raise CQ6-C by 5 points, since simultaneous high unemployment and high core inflation IS the stagflationary-squeeze signature, taking those points from B. Cut CQ3 P50 to ~1.2%/yr. Raise hypothesis plank (c) only if the inflation leg then rolls over, which this signpost does not by itself establish.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 1 fermi seat, effort 'labor'."}}